Revision history for Unblock-HTTP1

0.001  Development
    - Initial portable HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 protocol engine.
    - Event-loop, transport, and operating-system neutral byte interface.
    - Uniform::HTTP 0.04 request and response objects.
    - Strict request and response parsing with picohttpparser.
    - Fixed-length, chunked, and close-delimited body framing.
    - Lossless HTTP/1 trailer delivery into Uniform::HTTP messages.
    - Streaming request and response bodies with cooperative backpressure.
    - Informational responses and exact 101/CONNECT protocol handoff boundary.
    - HTTP/1 parity pass for CONNECT and Upgrade validation.
    - RFC-correct HEAD, 1xx, 204, 205, and 304 response framing.
    - Expect: 100-continue handling before application body delivery.
    - HTTP/1.0 persistence and close-delimited streaming parity.
    - EOF truncation, cancellation, early-final-response, and backpressure tests.
    - Expanded framing, fragmentation, queue/reuse, and configured-limit tests.
    - Cross-platform CI on Linux, macOS, and Windows with Perl 5.16 coverage.
    - Standalone parse, serialization, fixed-body, chunked-body, and loopback
      benchmarks for optimization work.
    - Fast paths for common HTTP/1.1 request and response serialization plans.
    - Buffered HTTP/1.1 planning fast paths benchmark at roughly four times the
      full validation-path controls in same-run development measurements.
    - Bodyless and fully buffered fixed-length receive fast paths avoid
      unnecessary receive-state allocation while preserving callback lifecycle.
    - Focused fast-path regressions compare coalesced and fragmented body input.
    - Cross-engine receive benchmarks isolate Uniform message construction as
      the clearest remaining receive-side performance cost.
    - Callback and message-finalization diagnostics show those lifecycle calls
      are not useful optimization targets.
    - Added an explicit distribution MANIFEST and CI disttest coverage.
    - Documented the portable adapter boundary and kept transport-specific
      native bridges outside the engine.
    - Originating response transfer-coding chains now mirror receive framing:
      final chunked is owned by Unblock and non-chunked final codings close.
    - Received absolute-form and CONNECT requests expose explicit Uniform
      routing metadata from the request-target while preserving raw Host bytes.
    - Hardened request-target form validation and HTTP method case sensitivity.
    - Rejected unsolicited bytes beyond a final client response boundary to
      prevent queued-response poisoning.
    - Added RFC-correct response framing for transfer codings that precede
      chunked or require connection-close delimiting.
    - Normalized equivalent Content-Length decimal spellings and rejected
      values outside the supported framing counter range.
    - Relaxed CONNECT Host validation so the Host port may be omitted while
      still requiring the same destination host.
    - Hardened serializer method, field, reason-phrase, status, and Connection
      syntax at the wire boundary, including duck-typed adapter objects.
    - Accepted higher HTTP/1 minor versions using HTTP/1.1 semantics while
      preserving the actual received version in Uniform messages.
    - Corrected HTTP/1.0 Expect and informational-response behavior.
    - Rejected unsolicited client response bytes both after a final response
      and while no request is outstanding.
    - Added general response Transfer-Encoding framing: final chunked framing
      is removed while earlier transfer codings remain opaque.
    - Kept strict CRLF requirements for chunk framing with dedicated tests.
    - Allowed legal empty Host port subcomponents while rejecting empty HTTP
      absolute-form hosts.
    - Kept informational responses on the dedicated send_informational() API.
    - Allowed Content-Length: 0 on CONNECT requests while still rejecting
      request content and Transfer-Encoding.
    - Enforced HTTP/1.1 TE negotiation, including Connection: TE,
      qvalues, implicit chunked acceptance, and response-coding selection.
    - Known outgoing trailer names are announced automatically with Trailer
      while late streaming trailers can still be predeclared explicitly.
    - Added a configurable cumulative chunk-extension budget in the vendored
      native decoder, defaulting to 16384 bytes per chunked message.
    - Engine constructors now reject unknown option names instead of silently
      ignoring configuration typos.
